💬 Done. Two commits, all four gates passing at each.

## 1. Cloud Run dev/prod suffix — verified and committed

This was already implemented in the working tree. I verified it end-to-end rather than assuming: a `resolve` job answers `prod`/`dev` and every GCP name carries it — service account, Cloud Run services, migration job, and the six Secret Manager entries (now twelve).

Two decisions in it are worth knowing, and both are right:
- **Containment, not equality.** A commit is prod when it's an ancestor of `vars.PRODUCTION_BRANCH`. Equality reads fine in a diff but the trigger is a `v*` tag — `refs/tags/v1.4.2` is never `refs/heads/main`, so a ref-name rule would have sent *every release* to dev.
- **The derivation lives in one composite action** (`.github/actions/gcp-auth`), not four inline copies, because a drifted copy there doesn't fail — it authenticates as the other environment.

## 2. Pending items by priority — the browser render

The top actionable item was rendering. Everything else in plan.md is blocked on a decision (the staff page needs "may an ops admin invite another ops admin?") or on hardware (Cloud Run needs a real GCP project). Meanwhile three changes had landed unrendered, **two of them fixed by arithmetic over two sheets** — which this repo records as the weaker method.

So I stood up Chromium (playwright + a private Debian sysroot, ~20 min outside the tree) and ran eight measurement groups over fourteen screens at six widths, plus an agent's phone round.

**The arithmetic held** — filter rows top-align everywhere, buttons are the design's 14px/9px with the 44px floor, the tab strip's 1px lap survives its clip, `DeskSelect`'s provisional option works, no overflow anywhere, contrast passes AA.

**It found one defect**, the same shape as all eleven found by the five earlier runs — a rule present, correct, attached to the wrong box:

`DayRangeForm` passed the window's hint as `TextField`'s `hint` on the **From** field. Hints render *inside* their field's box, so From stood a line taller than To, and `.report-range`'s `align-items: flex-end` drew **the two dates of one pair 22.8px apart at every width** — on the queue, the audit trail, and all six reports.

Two things I'd flag:
- **CLAUDE.md had the wrong half written down.** Its filter-row note names `.report-range` as the case where `flex-end` is "exactly right". It was the case where it was wrong. I corrected that line rather than only fixing the code.
- The same cause was an **accessibility** defect — all three callers word the hint as a sentence about leaving *both* bounds blank, so on the To field a screen reader said nothing.

The fix isn't the alignment (bottom-aligning is genuinely right once children are equal); it's that the hint belongs to the form. That needed `TextField` to *merge* a caller's `aria-describedby` rather than overwrite it — `{...rest}` is spread above the attribute, so a passed id was silently replaced, and on a field with neither error nor hint, replaced with `undefined`. Nobody passed one, which is why it never broke anything and why nothing caught it.

## 3. plan.md updated

Closed the tab-strip item (rendered, both halves correct) and the responsive item's open half. Recorded the new defect and, honestly, two things I chose *not* to do: `DayRangeForm` wraps to two lines between ~700–1200px (pre-existing, documented behaviour, a flex-distribution decision rather than a bug), and I added the harness-commit question as a real decision — six runs, eleven defects, **every one invisible to a green suite**, against CLAUDE.md's standing "out of scope for v1", with the fixtures rather than the browser being the part that would rot.

**Not verified here:** nothing in the Cloud Run path has touched GCP — no `gcloud`, no project. Service-account roles, Secret Manager entries, and whether a revision starts are still unknown. And `any-pointer: coarse` was Chromium's emulation agreeing with itself; a real touch device remains owed.

