All four gates pass and the harness reports 0 findings on the exact committed state. I staged the changes but did not commit — say the word if you want that.

## 1. Cloud Run dev/prod suffix — already done, and it's sound

Commit `0590a09` implemented it, and it's better than the ask. Two things worth knowing:

- **The rule is containment, not equality.** A commit is `prod` when it's an ancestor of `vars.PRODUCTION_BRANCH`. Equality would have been wrong: the trigger is a `v*` tag, and `refs/tags/v1.4.2` is never `refs/heads/main`, so a ref-name rule would have sent every release to dev while reading fine in the diff.
- **The suffix went on more than the service account** — Cloud Run services, the migration job and all six Secret Manager entries — which is what makes a single shared project safe, not just a two-project setup.

**One live gap I added to plan.md:** `vars.PRODUCTION_BRANCH` must be set on this repo before the first deploy. The default is `main` and **there is no `main` here** (branches are `development` and `master`). Unfetchable production branch resolves to `dev` — correct direction, completely silent. Every release would land on dev with a green workflow.

## 2. Pending items by priority

plan.md named its own top item: *"The render harness is still not committed, and that is now the biggest single lever here."* Six browser runs had found eleven defects and **the spec suite was green through all eleven** — every suite here runs in jsdom, which reports no geometry.

I took the middle of the three options plan.md laid out: committed `tools/render/` as a **manual script**, in nothing that gates. Two design choices answer the objections that had blocked it:

- **Fixtures can't rot silently.** `fixtures.ts` is typed against the `@udaan/shared` contracts and the root `typecheck` compiles it, so drift is a compile error naming the field. Not theoretical — the first compile rejected 11 fields across 5 fixtures.
- **Checks are properties, not expected values** — nothing to re-bless when a design changes on purpose. Each sweep is a historical defect generalised.

**Then I ran it over the screens plan.md named as the remaining gap** (4 reports + 4 drill-downs never drawn by any browser, + 2 registries). 126 findings → 0. Four real defects:

| Defect | Detail |
|---|---|
| `--color-warning` is gold-as-text | 2.76:1 on white, 2.50:1 on page. Hits the **invoice's short-pick rows** — the legal document. Gold is now two tokens: fill vs text (`#99611e`) |
| `optional` chip on `--color-text-faint` | 2.93:1, breaking the rule written at that token's own declaration. On every windowed screen |
| Supplier **name** ellipsed to ~45% | At 768/1024. Its own sheet had already computed the 137px and drawn the conclusion only for the cell next to it |
| `--on-brand` chip 56×34 at 390px | Also `apps/web`'s Account/Sign out — a phone client. Floor restored under `any-pointer: coarse` |

Three findings were **my harness being wrong**, not the desk — fixed in `checks.mjs` rather than reported, since a sweep that cries wolf gets ignored.

**Two honest caveats:** the `apps/web` half of the `--on-brand` fix is *reasoned, not rendered* (the harness serves `apps/admin` only), and `.order-detail__due` was found by inspection rather than render — that screen isn't in the harness's list.

## 3. plan.md updated

Closed the harness item and the drill-downs item; added the `PRODUCTION_BRANCH` gap; recorded one genuinely open decision: **`--color-warning` fails worse on the retailer client (2.16:1)**, and one of its call sites is the shared invoice — so fixing it there means deciding whether that document may differ by one colour across surfaces. I followed the cancelled-badge precedent (fix desk, leave phone, record it) rather than making a palette call from inside the code.

