## 1. Cloud Run service-account suffix — already done, verified

This shipped in `0590a09` and `94568be` before this session. I checked it against your wording rather than taking the commit message's word:

- `resolve` job answers one word, `prod` or `dev`, and `.github/actions/gcp-auth` puts it on the service account's **local part** (`udaan-deployer-prod@…`).
- The rule is **containment, not equality** — `git merge-base --is-ancestor` against `vars.PRODUCTION_BRANCH` (default `main`). That matters because the trigger is a `v*` tag: `refs/tags/v1.4.2` is not `refs/heads/main`, so a name-equality rule would have sent *every release* to dev while reading fine in the diff.
- The suffix also goes on the Cloud Run services, the migration job and the six Secret Manager entries — the account is the boundary only when dev and prod are separate projects.
- `cloud-run-deploy.spec.ts` pins all of it.

**One thing is still owed and I can't do it from here:** this repo has no `main` (branches are `development` and `master`), so on the default every release resolves to `dev`. Set `vars.PRODUCTION_BRANCH` to `development`. The workflow now raises a `::warning::` about this instead of failing silently, but the setting itself needs the Actions UI.

## 2. Pending items by priority

The board's top items were all decision- or hardware-blocked, so I took the highest-leverage thing that was neither: **the render harness now draws `apps/web`, which nothing had ever drawn.**

That was the largest item on its own "cannot answer" list, and it mattered because a fix shipped *the same day* — `Button`'s `--on-brand` 44px floor for Account/Sign out — had its `apps/web` half recorded as "reasoned rather than rendered."

Results, in IBM Plex Sans (the phone design's real face, so fit numbers are exact):

- **`apps/admin`: 0 findings** — the refactor moved nothing.
- **`apps/web`: 78** on ten screens at five widths.
- **The `--on-brand` fix is confirmed.** Account and Sign out are absent from the 390px target sweep.

Two findings were the harness being wrong, and I fixed those in `checks.mjs` rather than reporting them: visually-hidden elements aren't clipped text or targets, and a control inside a wrapping `<label>` is hit through the label.

The 78 reduce to two groups:

**Touch targets — eleven controls, and this is work, not a decision.** The precedent is settled three times over ("the design is the authority on what a surface looks like, not on what a thumb can reach"). The sharpest: **`apps/web`'s `SearchField` is `DeskSearchField`'s own 2026-09-12 defect, unfixed** — a 44px box around a 22px input, so 11px at each edge is drawn control that focuses nothing, on the first thing a shopkeeper touches. Also the cart's steppers at 32×30 and Add-to-cart at 86×33.

**Palette — six token pairings below AA, and this one I did not touch.** Your repo records twice that the retailer screens *are* the design and that changing that palette isn't a decision to make from inside the code, so I left it and wrote the numbers down instead.

## The decision I need from you

The old board tracked this as one token at 2.16:1. Rendering found six, and the worst isn't the one being tracked:

| Ratio | Where |
|---|---|
| 1.95 | order detail's "Due 2 Oct" |
| 2.16 | all three shortfall sentences — **including the invoice** |
| **3.80** | **`Place order · ₹38,827` — checkout's primary button, white on rust** |
| 2.91–4.46 | ~15 more across faint/muted/teal-tint |

Worth knowing: the shared invoice **already** renders in two different colours depending on which bundle drew it, because `desk.css` overrode `--color-warning` and `tokens.css` didn't. So "don't let the document diverge" argues *for* fixing the phone, not against.

> Should the phone palette take AA-corrected grades of its own hues, the way the desk's did?

Yes → six declarations in `tokens.css`; every screen reads them through tokens, so nothing else moves. I'd do the eleven touch targets in the same pass.

## 3. plan.md updated

Re-ranked to nine items. The honest headline changed: it used to open "almost nothing here is blocked on work" — that was true of what was *known*, and it isn't any more. New § "The retailer client" carries both tables with the measured numbers, and `CLAUDE.md` has the eighth run plus the corrected harness scope.

